<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Velama</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Velamas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Velamas are a Telugu-speaking landholding community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Velama warrior families served the Kakatiya dynasty, and after its fall the Recherla Velamas ruled Telangana from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rachakonda">Rachakonda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devarakonda">Devarakonda</a> in the 14th and 15th centuries. Later Velama chiefs held estates as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> under the Golconda Sultanate, the Nizams of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> and the British, among them Bobbili, Venkatagiri and Jatprole.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Velama is first recorded for the community in a 17th-century inscription, although the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinatha">Srinatha</a> used the terms Velama and Padmanayaka separately in the early 15th century. The historian Cynthia Talbot notes that in the Kakatiya period these were not closed kin groups: the Padmanayaka designation first appears in inscriptions around 1586 and was used by several Telugu warrior lineages, including those of the Recherla, Vipparla and Inigela clans, united by military service rather than shared ancestry. The term Velama appears only once in Kakatiya-era sources, for a collective of agriculturists.</p><p>A tradition recorded by Subba Rao in the early 20th century holds that Padmanayakas were originally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">Kapus</a>, or cultivators, who became Velamas and later gained Padmanayaka status through military service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Recherla dynasty ruled parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rachakonda">Rachakonda</a> (1335 to 1434) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devarakonda">Devarakonda</a> (1335 to 1475). After the Bahmani conquest some members moved south into the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and, granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> of Velugodu, became known as the Velugotis. The family established the Venkatagiri estate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a> district, and its cadet branches helped to found Pithapuram, Bobbili and Jatprole in the early 17th century. The Velugotis settled in Nellore in 1695.</p><p>Under the Golconda Sultanate some Velama chieftains became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a>, ranked by military and administrative service, which fostered rivalries. Notable lineages included the Appa Raos of Nuzvid and the Malraju clan of Narasaraopet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Talbot holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> identities such as Velama were shaped by social memory, constructed genealogies and retrospective claims to Kakatiya legitimacy rather than fixed bloodlines, and that genealogies composed by family bards reflect political motives. The Padmanayaka label was eventually absorbed into Velama. In the 19th century the Velugotis were often chosen as adoptive heirs by the Pithapuram and Bobbili Ranga Raos when male lines died out. Velamas are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, primarily of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> denomination.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Velama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jatprole was a hereditary estate held by the Surabhi family, <b>Velama</b> chiefs who had served earlier Telugu and Deccan rulers.</blockquote><a href="/state/jatprole-samasthan">Jatprole Samasthan</a></li><li><blockquote>The Recherla family were <b>Velama</b> Nayaka commanders who had served the Kakatiya dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/recherla-nayakas">Recherla Nayakas</a></li></ul></section>
